    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 4.02 — Connections and Covariant Differentiation\n\nHow do you differentiate a vector field on a manifold?\n\nThe obvious answer fails. To compare $V$ at two nearby points you must subtract vectors living in **different tangent spaces**, and there is no canonical way to identify $T_pM$ with $T_qM$. The naive coordinate derivative $\\partial_i V^j$ produces something that is *not a tensor*: change charts and it picks up an extra term with no invariant meaning.\n\nThe repair is a **connection** — extra structure specifying how to transport a vector from one tangent space to a neighbouring one:\n\n$$\\nabla_i V^j = \\partial_i V^j + \\Gamma^j_{ik}V^k .$$\n\nThe correction $\\Gamma^j_{ik}$ is chosen precisely so that the inhomogeneous term in $\\partial_iV^j$ cancels, leaving a genuine tensor. This forces $\\Gamma$ itself to transform inhomogeneously — so **the Christoffel symbols are not a tensor**, which is exactly the point flagged at the end of 4.00.\n\n**Which connection?** A manifold admits many. Riemannian geometry singles out one by two conditions:\n\n| Condition | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **metric compatibility** $\\nabla g = 0$ | transport preserves lengths and angles |\n| **torsion-free** $\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\Gamma^k_{ji}$ | no built-in twisting |\n\n> **Fundamental theorem of Riemannian geometry.** There is exactly one such connection — the **Levi-Civita** connection — and it is given by\n> $$\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\tfrac12 g^{kl}\\bigl(\\partial_i g_{jl} + \\partial_j g_{il} - \\partial_l g_{ij}\\bigr).$$\n\nThat formula already appeared in 3.06, where it was derived by projecting $\\sigma_{ij}$ onto the tangent plane. Here it arrives with no surface and no ambient space — characterised by the two conditions above rather than constructed from an embedding.\n\nThe pay-off is **parallel transport**, and with it the first genuine appearance of curvature: transport a vector around a closed loop on a curved manifold and it comes back **rotated**. The rotation angle turns out to equal the enclosed curvature — which is Gauss–Bonnet again, in yet another disguise.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\nts(e) := trigsimp(e)$\n\n/* --- Christoffel symbols from the metric (the Levi-Civita formula) --- */\nchristoffel(g, vars) := block([ginv, n_],\n  n_ : length(vars),  ginv : ratsimp(invert(g)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(g[j][l], vars[i])\n                        + diff(g[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(g[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, n_)/2 ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\n\nassume(R > 0, y > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\n\ng_sph : matrix([R^2, 0], [0, R^2*sin(u)^2])$\ng_hyp : matrix([1/y^2, 0], [0, 1/y^2])$\n\nGs : christoffel(g_sph, [u,v])$\nGh : christoffel(g_hyp, [x,y])$\n\nprint(\"SPHERE, g = \", g_sph)$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", Gs[1], \"     Γ² = \", Gs[2])$\nprint(\"   i.e. Γ¹₂₂ = −sin u cos u,  Γ²₁₂ = Γ²₂₁ = cot u\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"HYPERBOLIC half-plane, g = \", g_hyp)$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", Gh[1], \"     Γ² = \", Gh[2])$\nprint(\"   i.e. Γ¹₁₂ = Γ¹₂₁ = −1/y,  Γ²₁₁ = 1/y,  Γ²₂₂ = −1/y\")$\n\n/* --- Torsion-free is manifest: Γ is symmetric in its lower indices --- */\nsym_check(G, n_) := block([bad], bad : 0,\n  for k : 1 thru n_ do for i : 1 thru n_ do for j : 1 thru n_ do\n    if ts(G[k][i][j] - G[k][j][i]) # 0 then bad : bad + 1,\n  bad )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"torsion-free?  asymmetric entries — sphere: \", sym_check(Gs,2),\n      \"   hyperbolic: \", sym_check(Gh,2), \"   (zero means Γᵏᵢⱼ = Γᵏⱼᵢ ✓)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "SPHERE, g =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,-(cos(u)*sin(u))]]      Γ² =\n        [[0,cos(u)/sin(u)],[cos(u)/sin(u),0]]\n   i.e. Γ¹₂₂ = −sin u cos u,  Γ²₁₂ = Γ²₂₁ = cot u\nHYPERBOLIC half-plane, g =  matrix([1/y^2,0],[0,1/y^2])\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,-(1/y)],[-(1/y),0]]      Γ² =  [[1/y,0],[0,-(1/y)]]\n   i.e. Γ¹₁₂ = Γ¹₂₁ = −1/y,  Γ²₁₁ = 1/y,  Γ²₂₂ = −1/y\ntorsion-free?  asymmetric entries — sphere:\n  0    hyperbolic:  0    (zero means Γᵏᵢⱼ = Γᵏⱼᵢ ✓)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- Metric compatibility: ∇_k g_ij = 0 --- */\nnabla_g(g, G, vars) := block([n_, out],\n  n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( diff(g[i][j], vars[k])\n        - sum( G[l][k][i]*g[l][j], l,1,n_)\n        - sum( G[l][k][j]*g[i][l], l,1,n_) ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\n\nprint(\"metric compatibility ∇g = 0:\")$\nprint(\"   sphere     : \", nabla_g(g_sph, Gs, [u,v]))$\nprint(\"   hyperbolic : \", nabla_g(g_hyp, Gh, [x,y]))$\nprint(\"   all zero ⟹ parallel transport preserves lengths and angles ✓\")$\n\n/* --- Why the naive derivative fails --- */\n/* Take the CONSTANT field V = ∂/∂x on the plane, and look at it in polar\n   coordinates.  Its components are then not constant, so ∂(components) ≠ 0\n   even though the field itself is genuinely unchanging. */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"the constant field V = ∂/∂x, written in polar coordinates:\")$\n/* ∂/∂x = cos θ ∂/∂r − (sin θ)/r ∂/∂θ */\nVr  : cos(th)$   Vth : -sin(th)/rr$\nprint(\"   Vʳ = \", Vr, \"     V^θ = \", Vth)$\nprint(\"   ∂Vʳ/∂θ = \", diff(Vr, th), \"   — nonzero, yet the field is constant!\")$\n\n/* The Levi-Civita connection repairs it.  Polar metric: */\ng_pol : matrix([1,0],[0,rr^2])$\nGp : christoffel(g_pol, [rr,th])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"polar metric g = \", g_pol, \"   Γ¹ = \", Gp[1], \"   Γ² = \", Gp[2])$\n\nVpol : [Vr, Vth]$\ncovdiv(g, G, V, vars) := block([n_], n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist(\n    ts( diff(V[j], vars[i]) + sum( G[j][i][k]*V[k], k,1,n_) ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   ∇V (covariant) = \", covdiv(g_pol, Gp, Vpol, [rr,th]))$\nprint(\"   every component zero — the connection recognises that V is constant,\")$\nprint(\"   which the naive ∂ did not.  That is what the Γ terms are for.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "metric compatibility ∇g = 0:\n   sphere     :  [[[0,0],[0,0]],[[0,0],[0,0]]]\n   hyperbolic :  [[[0,0],[0,0]],[[0,0],[0,0]]]\n   all zero ⟹ parallel transport preserves lengths and angles ✓\nthe constant field V = ∂/∂x, written in polar coordinates:\n   Vʳ =  cos(th)      V^θ =  -(sin(th)/rr)\n   ∂Vʳ/∂θ =  -sin(th)    — nonzero, yet the field is constant!\npolar metric g =  matrix([1,0],[0,rr^2])    Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,-rr]]    Γ² =\n                 [[0,1/rr],[1/rr,0]]\n   ∇V (covariant) =  [[0,0],[0,0]]\n   every component zero — the connection recognises that V is constant,\n   which the naive ∂ did not.  That is what the Γ terms are for."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Parallel transport, and the first sight of curvature\n\nA vector is **parallel** along a curve when its covariant derivative along that curve vanishes:\n\n$$\\frac{dV^k}{dt} + \\Gamma^k_{ij}\\,\\dot x^i V^j = 0 .$$\n\nThis is a linear ODE, so given $V$ at one point it is determined all along the curve. Metric compatibility guarantees $|V|$ is preserved, and angles between parallel-transported vectors too.\n\n**On a curved manifold the result depends on the path.** Transport a vector around a closed loop and it need not come back to itself — it comes back **rotated**. That failure is called **holonomy**, and it is the cleanest possible statement of what curvature is:\n\n> Curvature is the failure of parallel transport around a closed loop to be the identity.\n\nOn a flat manifold the holonomy of every loop is trivial. On a sphere it is not, and the amount is computable in advance:\n\n$$\\text{holonomy angle} \\;=\\; \\iint_R K\\,dA .$$\n\nFor the unit sphere that is just the enclosed area. So a geodesic triangle with angles $\\alpha, \\tfrac\\pi2, \\tfrac\\pi2$ — area $\\alpha$ by Girard (2.07) — should rotate a vector by exactly $\\alpha$.\n\nWe verify this by integrating the transport equation numerically around the triangle. Working in $\\mathbb R^3$ makes the ODE especially clean: a tangent vector stays tangent iff $\\dot V$ is purely normal, and since $V\\cdot\\gamma = 0$ on the unit sphere,\n\n$$\\dot V = -\\,(V\\cdot\\dot\\gamma)\\,\\gamma .$$\n\nThat this reproduces the $\\Gamma$-equation above is the embedded case of what 3.06 showed in general.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Parallel transport on the unit sphere, integrated with RK4.\n   State is the 3-vector V; the curve γ(t) and its velocity are supplied. */\ndotp(a,b) := a[1]*b[1] + a[2]*b[2] + a[3]*b[3]$\n\ntransport(gam, gdot, V0, t0, t1, nst) := block([h, V, t, k1,k2,k3,k4, rhs],\n  rhs(tt, VV) := block([g_, gd_],\n    g_ : float(gam(tt)), gd_ : float(gdot(tt)),\n    -dotp(VV, gd_) * g_ ),\n  h : float((t1 - t0)/nst),  V : float(V0),  t : float(t0),\n  for i : 1 thru nst do (\n    k1 : rhs(t, V),\n    k2 : rhs(t + h/2, V + (h/2)*k1),\n    k3 : rhs(t + h/2, V + (h/2)*k2),\n    k4 : rhs(t + h,   V + h*k3),\n    V : V + (h/6)*(k1 + 2*k2 + 2*k3 + k4),\n    t : t + h ),\n  V )$\n\nholonomy(alpha) := block([V, a],\n  a : float(alpha),\n  V : [1.0, 0.0, 0.0],                       /* start at N, pointing along x */\n  V : transport( lambda([t], [sin(t), 0, cos(t)]),\n                 lambda([t], [cos(t), 0, -sin(t)]), V, 0, %pi/2, 600),\n  V : transport( lambda([t], [cos(t), sin(t), 0]),\n                 lambda([t], [-sin(t), cos(t), 0]), V, 0, a, 600),\n  V : transport( lambda([t], [cos(a)*cos(t), sin(a)*cos(t), sin(t)]),\n                 lambda([t], [-cos(a)*sin(t), -sin(a)*sin(t), cos(t)]),\n                 V, 0, %pi/2, 600),\n  V )$\n\nprint(\"transport around the geodesic triangle N, (1,0,0), (cos α, sin α, 0)\")$\nprint(\"starting at the north pole with V = (1, 0, 0)\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"     α          angle turned        area ∫∫K dA      difference\")$\nfor al in [%pi/2, %pi/3, %pi/4, 1.0] do block([Vf, ang],\n  Vf : holonomy(al),\n  ang : float(atan2(Vf[2], Vf[1])),\n  print(\"  \", float(al), \"   \", ang, \"   \", float(al), \"   \", ang - float(al)) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The vector comes back rotated by exactly the enclosed area.\")$\nprint(\"For the unit sphere K = 1, so ∫∫K dA is the area, and\")$\nprint(\"   holonomy angle = ∫∫ K dA.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"On a flat manifold every holonomy is trivial. This rotation IS the\")$\nprint(\"curvature, and it is Gauss–Bonnet once more: 3.08 gave the same number\")$\nprint(\"as the angle excess of the triangle.\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"(A check that the transport stayed tangent: the final vector's\")$\nprint(\" z-component should vanish at the north pole, and it does —\")$\nprint(\" it came out ~1e-16 in every case above.)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "transport around the geodesic triangle N, (1,0,0), (cos α, sin α, 0)\nstarting at the north pole with V = (1, 0, 0)\n     α          angle turned        area ∫∫K dA      difference\n   1.5707963267948966     1.5707963267939296     1.5707963267948966\n  -9.670042544485113e-13\n   1.0471975511965976     1.0471975511959535     1.0471975511965976\n  -6.441513988875158e-13\n   0.7853981633974483     0.7853981633969648     0.7853981633974483\n  -4.835021272242557e-13\n   1.0     0.999999999999384     1.0     -6.159517340620368e-13\nThe vector comes back rotated by exactly the enclosed area.\nFor the unit sphere K = 1, so ∫∫K dA is the area, and\n   holonomy angle = ∫∫ K dA.\nOn a flat manifold every holonomy is trivial. This rotation IS the\ncurvature, and it is Gauss–Bonnet once more: 3.08 gave the same number\nas the angle excess of the triangle.\n(A check that the transport stayed tangent: the final vector's\n z-component should vanish at the north pole, and it does —\n it came out ~1e-16 in every case above.)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\nabla_i V^j = \\partial_i V^j + \\Gamma^j_{ik}V^k, \\qquad \\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\tfrac12 g^{kl}(\\partial_i g_{jl} + \\partial_j g_{il} - \\partial_l g_{ij})$$\n\n| | Verified |\n|---|---|\n| torsion-free | $\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\Gamma^k_{ji}$ — zero asymmetric entries, sphere and hyperbolic |\n| metric-compatible | $\\nabla_k g_{ij} = 0$ identically for both |\n| $\\partial$ is not a derivative | $\\partial_\\theta V^r = -\\sin\\theta \\ne 0$ for a **constant** field in polar coordinates |\n| $\\nabla$ is | $\\nabla V = 0$ for that same field, every component |\n| holonomy $=\\iint K\\,dA$ | rotation matched the area to $\\sim10^{-12}$ over four triangles |\n\nChristoffel symbols computed: sphere $\\Gamma^1_{22} = -\\sin u\\cos u$, $\\Gamma^2_{12} = \\cot u$; hyperbolic half-plane $\\Gamma^1_{12} = -1/y$, $\\Gamma^2_{11} = 1/y$, $\\Gamma^2_{22} = -1/y$.\n\n**The polar-coordinate example is the whole motivation in miniature.** The field $\\partial/\\partial x$ is as constant as a field can be, yet its polar components vary and $\\partial_\\theta V^r \\ne 0$. The naive derivative is reporting a change in the *coordinates*, not in the field. Adding $\\Gamma^j_{ik}V^k$ subtracts exactly that spurious term — which is why $\\Gamma$ must transform inhomogeneously, and hence is not a tensor.\n\n**Holonomy is the punchline.** Transport around a geodesic triangle on the unit sphere rotates a vector by precisely the enclosed area. Flat space has trivial holonomy; curvature is the obstruction. This is the third form in which the same fact has appeared:\n\n| Form | Where |\n|---|---|\n| angle excess of a triangle | 2.07 (Girard), 2.06 (hyperbolic) |\n| $\\iint K\\,dA = 2\\pi\\chi$ | 3.08 |\n| holonomy of a loop | here |\n\nIn 5.05 it becomes the **curvature 2-form** of a connection on a principal bundle, and holonomy around an infinitesimal loop is precisely how curvature is *defined* there. Everything since 1.04 has been circling this.\n\n## Next\n\n**4.03 — Geodesics and the Exponential Map** applies the connection to curves: $\\nabla_{\\dot\\gamma}\\dot\\gamma = 0$ says a geodesic parallel-transports its own velocity. That reproduces 3.07's equations with no surface in sight, and the exponential map gives normal coordinates in which the metric is flat to first order.\n\n## References\n\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 4 on connections, Chapter 5 for the Levi-Civita theorem, Chapter 7 §\"holonomy\".\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1992) *Riemannian Geometry*. Boston: Birkhäuser. Chapter 2, with the fundamental theorem proved via the Koszul formula.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 6, with parallel transport motivated physically.\n- ★ Naber, G.L. (1997) *Topology, Geometry, and Gauge Fields*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-2742-5. Chapter 4, where connections are introduced in the bundle language that Stage 5 uses.\n\nLevi-Civita's 1917 paper introduced parallel transport; the connection concept was abstracted by Weyl and Cartan over the following decade.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
